OpenSSH's ssh, sftp and scp for DOS (DJGPP, 32-bit, need DPMI: vmdos's
own), in C:\VMDOS\NET on C: only (the boot floppy has no room).

    STARTNET                         (once: the TCP/IP settings, by DHCP)
    SSH user@host
    SSH host                         (asks "login as:"; SET USER=name skips it)
    SSH -p 2222 user@192.168.1.10    SSH -6 user@host   SSH user@2001:db8::10
    SSH -i C:\HOME\.ssh\id_ed25519 user@host uptime
    SFTP user@host                   (get, put, ls, cd, lcd, ... ; bye)
    SCP C:\NOTES.TXT user@host:      SCP user@host:file.tgz C:\DL

  bin/SSH.EXE        github.com/openssh/openssh-portable tag V_10_6_P2, the
                     clients only (SFTP.EXE and SCP.EXE: below), with OpenSSL
                     3.6.5's libcrypto (tools/build-openssl.sh, no-sock and
                     without what ssh doesn't use): RSA, ECDSA and Ed25519 keys
                     and host keys (RSA in OpenSSH's format or old PEM, with a
                     passphrase or not), AES-GCM/CTR/CBC and ChaCha20-Poly1305,
                     ML-KEM/sntrup/Curve25519, ECDH and Diffie-Hellman key
                     exchange; on vmdos's own TCP (below); DJGPP gcc 12, UPX
                     --best. libcrypto's seed is getentropy() and its clock
                     ossl_dos_time() and the rest, both dos-compat.c's.
  dos-compat.h       forced into every OpenSSH file: what DJGPP lacks or gets
                     in the way (signals, mmap, IXANY, ...), and the renames below
  dos-compat.c       getentropy(), poll()/ppoll(), fcntl(), gettimeofday()/time(),
                     gmtime(), stat(), strerror() for network errors, Ctrl-C
                     as a character in raw mode, the login prompt
  dos-sock.c         sockets and DNS on vmdos's TCP (below)
  dos-term.c         the terminal (below)
  dos-co.c, dos-spawn.c   ssh inside sftp and scp (below)
  dos-res.c          "no" from the resolver library (SSHFP records)
  openssh-dos.patch  the changes to OpenSSH itself: the user name prompt;
                     sftp and scp start the built-in ssh instead of fork();
                     C:FILE and C:\DIR\FILE are local paths to scp
  djstrerr.py        for the build: DJGPP's errno numbers, for the network
                     errno list made with Watt-32's headers
  LICENCE.openssh    OpenSSH's licence (BSD-style)

Networking. vmdos runs the TCP connections itself, on the PC's network
card (src/tcp9p.cpp, as for VMDOSNET's 9P drives), IPv4 and IPv6; the
programs ask it through INT 2Fh AX=564Eh BL=13-18 (connect, send,
receive, status, close, and one UDP question and answer for DNS). No
packet driver or DOS TCP/IP stack is involved, and nothing runs DHCP: the
addresses, netmask, gateways and DNS servers are vmdos's TCP/IP settings
(%MTCPCFG%, C:\VMDOS\NET\VMTCP.CFG: IPADDR, NETMASK, GATEWAY, NAMESERVER,
IPV6ADDR, IPV6GATEWAY, IPV6NAMESERVER), as STARTNET's DHCP (and SLAAC) or
VMTCP set them. With no address there the programs say to run STARTNET.
vmdos finds the next hop's MAC (ARP, neighbor solicitation) and answers for
the addresses while a connection is open. Names are looked up with
AAAA and A questions to the DNS servers in turn; with a global IPv6
address, IPv6 addresses are tried first. Not there: listening sockets (no
port forwarding, -L/-R/-D), reverse DNS, UDP. Kernel option tcplog logs
the connections and DNS questions (DMESG).

Files. HOME is C:\HOME unless set; ~/.ssh is C:\HOME\.ssh (a long name:
DOSLFN, which AUTOEXEC.BAT loads, has to be there): known_hosts, id_rsa,
id_ecdsa or id_ed25519, config. ssh-keygen isn't built: make the key
elsewhere and copy it there.
Its permissions aren't checked: DOS has none, and DJGPP's stat() makes up
0644 (0444 if read-only), which ssh found "too open" for any key.
The system-wide ssh_config is C:\VMDOS\SSH\SSH_CONFIG if you make one.
C:\HOME\.ssh\SSHSEED.BIN keeps random state between runs.

Randomness. DOS has no /dev/urandom: getentropy() takes RDSEED/RDRAND (any
CPU of the last ten years), the seed file, the clocks, and TSC-against-8254
timing jitter (lots of it when the CPU has no RDRAND), all through SHA-512.

The terminal. DOS has no terminal emulator (ANSI.SYS at best), so ssh has
one: TERM=linux is sent unless TERM is set in DOS, and the screen behaves as
the Linux console (which is the PC text screen anyway): colors (256 and RGB
colors to the nearest of 16; 8 background colors), cursor movement, erase,
insert/delete of characters and lines, scroll regions, line drawing, the
alternate screen (xterm's), UTF-8 shown in code page 437, the screen's real
size (25 or 50 lines, ...). Keys: arrows, Home/End/Ins/Del/PgUp/PgDn, F1-F12,
Alt+key as Esc+key, Backspace as DEL; with TERM=xterm (SET TERM=xterm) the
xterm sequences instead. Tested: bash, top, vi, colored ls. Ctrl-C goes to
the remote side; ~. ends a stuck session as usual.

sftp and scp. They normally start ssh as another process and talk to it
through pipes; DOS runs one program at a time. So SFTP.EXE and SCP.EXE
carry ssh inside: it runs as a coroutine on its own stack, joined to sftp
or scp by pipes in memory, each side running while the other waits.
Everything else is as usual: the same options and config, passwords and
host keys asked as ssh asks them, the progress meter. Paths: scp takes
C:\DIR\FILE; in sftp, backslashes escape, so write C:/DIR/FILE. Files are
copied as they are (binary). sftp's lls is DOS's DIR and ! runs
COMMAND.COM (%COMSPEC%; ! alone: a DOS prompt, EXIT returns): DOS has no
fork(), so they are run as DOS runs one program from another, sftp
waiting; the connection isn't served meanwhile, so keep them short. Not there: scp -3 (remote to remote through
here: two ssh at once), sftp -S/-D (other programs), local-to-local scp.

Not there: ssh-agent and agent forwarding to a DOS agent, ControlMaster,
ProxyCommand and anything else that starts a program (DOS can't run one
beside ssh), X11 forwarding, PKCS#11/FIDO keys, port forwarding (no listening sockets).

tools/build-openssh.sh rebuilds bin/SSH.EXE, SFTP.EXE and SCP.EXE (DJGPP
cross gcc; it fetches OpenSSH and Watt-32 at the pinned versions: Watt-32
gives the build its socket headers, and OpenSSH's configure something to
find; none of its code is in the programs).

Prompts (login as, password, yes/no, sftp>) are edited by the terminal
too (DJGPP's own line editing showed ^H and ^M): Left/Right, Home/End
(Ctrl-A/E), Ctrl+Left/Right by words, Backspace, Del, Ctrl-K, Ctrl-U or
Esc, Enter; Up/Down go through the last 32 lines typed (not passwords);
Tab at sftp> completes commands and remote/local paths as sftp does with
libedit (its own code, sftp.c). DJGPP's termios puts its own console line
reader in front of stdio once ssh asks about the terminal; it's taken out
again (dos-term.c), or sftp> got that one: no arrows, and while it waited
for a key the ssh inside didn't run, so after a minute at the prompt vmdos
closed the connection (src/tcp9p.cpp: a DOS program stopped asking about
it) and the next command said "Transport endpoint is not connected".

Things found on the way, worked around here: DJGPP's select() and
gettimeofday() go through mktime(), which sometimes loops for ever in
timesub() (ssh hung after login); and INT 10h AX=1003h (blink/intensity)
froze the guest under vmdos now and then, so ssh leaves the blink bit alone;
DJGPP's localtime() got dates wrong (sftp's ls), so gmtime/localtime are
plain arithmetic here (the DOS clock is taken as UTC); DJGPP's file type
bits aren't POSIX's (S_IFREG is 0), so stat() results are converted.
DJGPP's basename() gives "" for "dir/" (POSIX: "dir"), and sftp's glob
marks directories with a /, so get -r and mget -r made a local directory
named "" (mkdir: ENOENT): basename() here is POSIX's.
